{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-116180",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f3b0dad3f1cb4b2216e3fce7f1fefa8beac15a6e16ab09ab3a4c47a2472e2f9a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(y+3)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}={y}^{2}(y+3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-3$ | $-3<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(y+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}.$",
